WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:03.040
(bright music)

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:06.829
- When I think about the
wilderness of Denali,

00:00:06.829 --> 00:00:10.357
I think of vastness and how small we are

00:00:10.357 --> 00:00:13.688
in that great big wintry world.

00:00:13.688 --> 00:00:16.098
(dog howls)

00:00:16.098 --> 00:00:18.509
Sled dogs are not the
National Park Service's idea,

00:00:18.509 --> 00:00:21.738
this is an idea that we have
adopted from everyone else

00:00:21.738 --> 00:00:24.155
who knew that dog teams
were just the best,

00:00:24.155 --> 00:00:28.149
most reliable, and I'd
argue most fun way to travel

00:00:28.149 --> 00:00:30.149
around Alaska in winter.

00:00:36.848 --> 00:00:39.075
So I'm the kennels manager
in Denali National Park

00:00:39.075 --> 00:00:41.985
and Preserve, and I'm
in charge of the only

00:00:41.985 --> 00:00:46.152
sled dog kennel in the
entire National Park Service.

00:00:47.038 --> 00:00:51.139
In 1980, the original park
boundary was designated

00:00:51.139 --> 00:00:54.686
as wilderness, and
officially-designated wilderness

00:00:54.686 --> 00:00:58.038
is the highest level of federal
land protection available.

00:00:58.038 --> 00:01:00.198
And it means that you can't use any motorized

00:01:00.198 --> 00:01:02.015
form of transport to travel.

00:01:02.015 --> 00:01:03.599
(dogs bark)

00:01:03.599 --> 00:01:06.167
It's all gonna be through
non-motorized transport

00:01:06.167 --> 00:01:10.737
so dog teams, skis,
snowshoes, anything like that.

00:01:10.737 --> 00:01:12.537
Let me see your teeth.

00:01:12.537 --> 00:01:15.567
These dogs are all Alaskan Huskies.

00:01:15.567 --> 00:01:19.148
Born and raised for
thousands of years to live in

00:01:19.148 --> 00:01:22.716
the harsh winter conditions
of Alaska and to want to run

00:01:22.716 --> 00:01:23.996
and pull a sled.

00:01:23.996 --> 00:01:24.913
Ready?
Hike!

00:01:27.258 --> 00:01:30.087
Asking me my favorite dog
is like asking a parent

00:01:30.087 --> 00:01:31.236
their favorite child.

00:01:31.236 --> 00:01:32.527
(laughs)

00:01:32.527 --> 00:01:34.927
It's not possible to answer that question.

00:01:34.927 --> 00:01:38.535
They all win my heart
in some way or another.

00:01:38.535 --> 00:01:43.476
These guys are amazing; they
bridge the gap to the wild.

00:01:43.476 --> 00:01:46.655
I think the dogs are just
an incredible invitation

00:01:46.655 --> 00:01:49.956
and inspiration for our visitors.

00:01:49.956 --> 00:01:52.356
We're all a part of this
wilderness, we're all

00:01:52.356 --> 00:01:56.276
a part of this place, and
it has so much to offer us

00:01:56.276 --> 00:02:00.116
if we're willing to go out and
be challenged by it a little,

00:02:00.116 --> 00:02:02.366
to immerse ourselves in it.

00:02:04.469 --> 00:02:06.802
(dog barks)

